sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch11-21> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The minutely branched, arborescent outline superposed on the lobes and saddles of some ammonite sutures, with branches ending in rounded arcs, is a surface-tension phenomenon of the same kind as the patterns made by milk on a greasy plate or oil on an alkaline solution.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "The fine frilling of ammonite sutures is a surface-tension phenomenon";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter XI, “The Logarithmic Spiral”; topic “Septa of chambered shells”. Scope: ammonoids with complex (ammonitic) suture lines"
}
